\usepackage{enumitem}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}
\title{Questionnaire Design for a Two-Wave Household Panel Survey}
\author{Fieldwork manual --- K.~Osei}
\date{May 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Overview}
The second wave of the household panel reuses the core modules
from the baseline instrument and adds a short module on
informal caregiving.
This note documents the layout conventions used in the
printed questionnaire and in the interviewer manual.

\section{Conventions}
Filter questions are typeset as enumerated sub-items,
and the response lists use compact bullets:
\begin{enumerate}
\item How many people usually live in this household?
\item Does anyone in the household provide regular unpaid care
to a relative or neighbour?
\end{enumerate}

The instruction boxes collect the interviewer prompts:
\begin{itemize}
\item Read every response option aloud.
\item Probe once when the answer is uncertain.
\end{itemize}

\section{Pilot Findings}
In the pilot interviews the compact lists shortened the
average module time by about one minute,
and interviewers reported fewer skipped filters than
in the baseline layout.
The conventions above are therefore kept for the main fieldwork.

\section{Conclusion}
The instrument is ready for the main wave once the layout
conventions are applied consistently across all modules.
\end{document}

